<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="66694" href="schedule/2016/fall/GLBL/296/66694.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2016" href="schedule/2016.xml">2016</calendarYear><term id="120168" href="schedule/2016/fall.xml">Fall 2016</term><subject id="GLBL" href="schedule/2016/fall/GLBL.xml">Global Studies</subject><course id="296" href="schedule/2016/fall/GLBL/296.xml">Global St Foundation Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>SL </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Technology &amp; Social Movements</sectionTitle><sectionDateRange>Meets 22-Aug-16 - 14-Oct-16.</sectionDateRange><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course examines the use of technology by social movements in order to affect change at the local, national, and global levels. We will focus on the role of internet-based communications tools such as social networking in overcoming resource disparities, the effectiveness of virtual protest, and the subversion of mainstream content by opposition groups. The course will include theoretical, case study, and research elements.</sectionText><partOfTerm>A</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2016-08-22Z</startDate><endDate>2016-10-14Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>10:50 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>T      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>134</roomNumber><buildingName>Armory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Wedig" firstName="T">Wedig, T</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>